---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/nutrition-dietetics/foundational/nutrients-and-how-they-work">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first lesson in Nutrition and Dietetics — the discipline that connects the
    food on your plate to the chemistry inside your cells. You need nothing from earlier
    lessons; everything here starts from everyday food. What the lesson builds is a
    vocabulary: six classes of nutrients, each with a job, a number and a characteristic
    way of failing. Every dietary argument you will ever read — the honest ones and the
    fashionable ones alike — is, underneath, a claim about these classes and how they
    interact.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the nutrient classes and say what each one does — and what it cannot do;</li>
    <li>explain the 4, 4 and 9 kcal/g figures and where the "calorie" on a food label comes from;</li>
    <li>tell essential from non-essential nutrients, and why "essential" means the body cannot make it;</li>
    <li>explain why fat-soluble vitamins can be stored to the point of harm while water-soluble ones largely cannot;</li>
    <li>reason about water and electrolytes quantitatively, including what sweat actually takes away;</li>
    <li>read an energy-balance calculation like a dietitian — as direction and magnitude, not prophecy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit to carry through the subject: nutrients are not moral characters — no food is
    "clean" or "evil". There are requirements, windows and balances, and the discipline's
    job is to keep those quantitative rather than emotional.
  </p>

  <h2>What food does inside the body</h2>
  <p>
    Nutrition is the study of what food does inside the body. Its vocabulary is the
    nutrients: <strong>macronutrients</strong> — carbohydrates, proteins and fats — supply
    energy and building material; <strong>micronutrients</strong> — vitamins and minerals —
    run the chemistry; and water is the medium everything happens in.
  </p>
  <p>
    The discipline's first sorting move is the energy question. Macronutrients supply
    metabolisable energy, measured in kilocalories (kcal — the "calorie" of food labels is
    actually a kilocalorie): about 4 kcal per gram for carbohydrate, 4 for protein and 9
    for fat (alcohol, though not a nutrient, adds 7). Micronutrients supply essentially no
    energy but make energy use possible at all. The analogy that survives every physiology
    course: macronutrients are the fuel and the building materials; micronutrients are the
    spark plugs and gaskets; water is the cooling system and the oil. An engine can have a
    full tank and still run badly if the gaskets are gone — which is exactly how deficiency
    diseases work in a well-fed but unbalanced diet.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="guess the magnitude — the brain's daily fuel" widget="estimate" config={nutGlucoseEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> six classes, one sorting question (does it supply energy?),
    and one engine analogy that will keep earning its keep. The widget walks the classes;
    the sections below give each its mechanism, its numbers and its failure modes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the nutrient classes and what each is for" widget="equationStep" config={macroCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the first three steps share the unit (kcal/g) and the
    last two deliberately break it. That is the macro/micro division made visible:
    macronutrients are counted in grams and kilocalories, while micronutrients are counted
    in milligrams and micrograms because the body needs them in catalytic amounts — enough
    to run the chemistry, not enough to matter on the energy ledger. Water rounds out the
    list as the nutrient with no energy at all and no substitutes.
  </p>

  <h2>Macronutrients: fuel and fabric</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Carbohydrate</strong> is the preferred fuel: digestible starches and sugars are
    broken down to glucose, which fuels the muscles quickly and is the brain's
    near-exclusive substrate — the body burns roughly 120 g of glucose a day, most of it
    for the brain. Surplus carbohydrate is stored as <strong>glycogen</strong> — about
    400–500 g in liver and muscle, roughly a day's reserve — and beyond that, converted to
    fat. <strong>Fibre</strong> is the carbohydrate we digest by not digesting it: soluble
    fibre slows absorption and feeds gut bacteria, whose fermentation products (short-chain
    fatty acids) are themselves absorbed fuels and signals; insoluble fibre accelerates
    transit. The common recommendation of 25–30 g a day or more is less about roughage than
    about feeding the microbiome and slowing the glucose wave.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Protein</strong> is the body's fabric: twenty amino acids, of which nine are
    <strong>essential</strong> — a technical term meaning the body cannot synthesise them,
    so they must come from food. Protein builds and repairs, and the body keeps no storage
    tank for it, so supply must be daily; quality is about the essential profile (a
    "complete" protein supplies all nine, and plant sources can be combined to the same
    effect). Requirements scale with body mass and use: the reference intake is about 0.8
    g per kilogram of body weight per day for a sedentary adult, perhaps 1.2–2.0 for
    athletes and the recovering ill. Surplus protein is not stored as muscle — its carbon
    is burned or converted, and its nitrogen is excreted as urea by the kidney, which is
    why mega-dosing has no storage benefit and some metabolic cost.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="fill the blanks — the protein rules" widget="cloze" config={nutProteinCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>Fat</strong> is the dense fuel (9 kcal/g — the reason energy-dense foods are
    usually fatty), the carrier for vitamins A, D, E and K, the stuff of every membrane,
    and the raw material of hormones. Two fatty acids are essential: linoleic (omega-6) and
    alpha-linolenic (omega-3) — the longer-chain omega-3s (EPA and DHA, from oily fish) are
    conditionally required and drive much of the dietary-fat debate. Stored fat (adipose
    tissue) is the body's true energy tank: about 9,000 kcal per kilogram against glycogen's
    roughly 2,000 in total — the evolutionary explanation for why humans can go weeks
    without food but only days without water. Digestion matters here too: fat is emulsified
    by bile and hydrolysed by lipase, and its absorption is slow enough to extend satiety —
    one of the mechanisms behind "fat keeps you full".
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="drag between the two fuel tanks" widget="compare" config={nutTanksCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "carbs make you fat; fat makes you fat" — as though one
  macronutrient were the culprit. It feels right because each diet trend needs a villain.
  But chronic weight gain requires a sustained energy surplus, whatever its source;
  macronutrients differ in <em>how easily</em> they drive surplus (energy density, satiety,
  palatability) and in health effects at equal energy — not in their power to violate
  energy balance. The diet wars are about adherence and cardiometabolic quality, not
  metabolism-breaking magic.</p>

  <h2>Vitamins and minerals: the machinery's chemistry</h2>
  <p>
    Vitamins are organic molecules needed in milligram or microgram amounts because the
    body cannot make them, or cannot make enough. The functional grouping is fat-soluble
    (<strong>A, D, E, K</strong> — absorbed with dietary fat and stored in liver and fat
    tissue) versus water-soluble (<strong>C</strong> and the <strong>B family</strong> —
    little stored, the excess leaves in urine, so they are needed regularly). The grouping
    has practical teeth in both directions. Storage means fat-soluble vitamins can
    accumulate to harmful levels — toxicity is a real risk at high supplement doses. And
    water-solubility means delicacy in the kitchen: several B vitamins and vitamin C are
    destroyed by heat and leach into cooking water, which is why steaming or grilling
    vegetables preserves more of them than boiling.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each fact by solubility" widget="sort-buckets" config={nutVitaminSort} />
  <p>
    The mechanism story is the point. B vitamins are <em>coenzymes</em>: thiamine (B1) sits
    at the centre of glucose metabolism (deficiency: beriberi), niacin is part of NAD
    (deficiency: pellagra), folate and B12 carry one-carbon units for DNA synthesis
    (deficiency: megaloblastic anaemia — and folate insufficiency in early pregnancy causes
    neural-tube defects, which is why flour is fortified). Vitamin C is a cofactor for
    collagen maturation (deficiency: scurvy — bleeding gums, failing wounds, exactly the
    collagen story). Vitamin D is really a <em>pre-hormone</em>, made in skin under UV
    light and completed by liver and kidney, that controls calcium absorption.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each vitamin with its job and its deficiency" widget="match" config={nutCofactorMatch} />
  <p>
    Minerals are inorganic elements, and their logic is elements-in-structure or
    elements-in-chemistry. <strong>Calcium</strong> (about 1 kg in the body) is scaffold
    and signal — bones, but also the ion that triggers every muscle contraction.
    <strong>Iron</strong> sits in haemoglobin's haem and in mitochondrial cytochromes;
    deficiency (the world's most common micronutrient deficiency — anaemia, fatigue) and
    overload (a poisoned liver; also genetic haemochromatosis) are both diseases, because
    the body controls iron by <em>absorption</em> alone, having no excretion route worth
    the name. <strong>Iodine</strong> is built into thyroid hormones (deficiency: goitre;
    in pregnancy, the leading preventable cause of intellectual disability — which is why
    iodised salt is one of public health's great wins). The discipline's key insight:
    micronutrients run in <em>windows</em> — both ends of every nutrient curve are disease.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> vitamins are harmless because they are natural, so more
  is always better. It seems reasonable — deficiency is the famous danger, and fruits and
  vegetables never hurt anyone. But the dose makes the poison: fat-soluble vitamins are
  stored, and chronic megadoses of A or D cause documented organ damage, while high-dose
  supplements of others can interfere with medications or mask deficiencies (high folate
  can hide B12 deficiency while the nerve damage progresses). Contrast the two delivery
  routes: nutrients inside foods arrive in the company of fibre, water and co-factors at
  doses the body evolved to handle; concentrated pills do not.</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — the dose makes the poison" widget="judge" config={nutMegaJudge} />

  <h2>Water and electrolytes: the medium and its current</h2>
  <p>
    Water is the medium for everything above — 50–65% of body mass, the solvent of blood
    and cytoplasm, the reactant of hydrolysis, the coolant of sweating. Losses of just a
    few percent of body water measurably degrade performance and mood, and losses beyond
    about 10% are life-threatening — which is why water, not food, sets the survival clock.
    <strong>Electrolytes</strong> — sodium, potassium, chloride and their partners — give
    that water its properties: they set <em>osmolarity</em> (water follows solutes, so the
    body's water compartments track salt), <em>membrane voltage</em> (nerve impulses and
    heartbeats are sodium and potassium currents), and <em>fluid volume</em> (blood
    pressure responds to sodium).
  </p>
  <p>
    The quantitative picture: the body holds roughly 42 L of water in a 70 kg adult — about
    28 L inside cells, 14 outside (blood plasma plus interstitial fluid) — and sodium
    guards the outside while potassium guards the inside; the gradients between them,
    maintained by pumps, <em>are</em> the electrochemistry of nerve and muscle. Worked
    example, the athlete: an hour of hard exercise in heat can shed about a litre of
    sweat, carrying roughly a gram of sodium with it. Replacing water but not sodium can,
    in extreme cases, <em>dilute</em> the remaining body sodium — exercise-associated
    hyponatraemia, the documented cause of marathon deaths from over-drinking — while
    replacing neither degrades performance within minutes. The everyday corollary: most
    dietary advice recommends keeping sodium moderate (high intake is associated with
    raised blood pressure in salt-sensitive people) while raising potassium (fruit,
    vegetables) — the two ions pull blood pressure in opposite directions, and modern
    diets are usually wrong on both.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="place the marker — the body's water map" widget="place" config={nutWaterPlace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "you need eight glasses a day regardless" and "sweating
  means you need sports drinks". Both sound like science but neither survives the
  arithmetic. Water needs vary widely — food itself supplies about 20% of typical fluid
  intake, and thirst is a workable guide in healthy people — and electrolyte replacement
  matters in proportion to sweat volume and duration: an hour at the gym is water, not
  marketing; a six-hour summer shift is both.</p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="walk the over-drinking case" widget="branch" config={nutSweatBranch} />

  <h2>Energy balance, made quantitative</h2>
  <p>
    The body obeys thermodynamics. Expenditure is mostly your basal metabolic rate — the
    cost of simply being alive (breathing, heartbeat, ion pumping, protein turnover) —
    multiplied by an activity factor. Balance is intake minus expenditure, and it
    integrates daily: a sustained surplus is stored, a sustained deficit is drawn from
    reserves. The panel below lets you drag the three numbers yourself.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="intake vs expenditure — drag the sliders" widget="sliderLab" config={balanceCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> read the lab like a dietitian reading a case.
    Expenditure = BMR × activity (1650 × 1.55 ≈ 2558 kcal at the defaults), so a surplus
    of 200 kcal a day — a biscuit's worth — integrates to about 73,000 kcal a year,
    potentially some 9 kg of adipose tissue if nothing else adjusts. But notice the word
    "if": in reality the body <em>does</em> adjust — metabolism adapts partially to
    deficit and spontaneous activity shifts — which is why the lab's arithmetic is a first
    approximation (the famous "3500 kcal per pound" rule overstates long-term change by
    ignoring it), not a law. The professional use of the equation is direction and
    magnitude, not prophecy; and BMR itself scales with lean body mass, another reason
    protein adequacy and resistance activity matter.
  </p>

  <h2>From nutrients to diet: the practice of the discipline</h2>
  <p>
    People eat foods, not nutrients, and foods deliver their nutrients in packages — with
    fibre, matrices and interactions that change what the body actually absorbs. Nutrients
    work together: each one matters most in the company the diet keeps. That gap between
    nutrient chemistry and dietary pattern is where most public confusion lives — and
    where the discipline of dietetics does its work: assessment first, then counselling
    grounded in evidence rather than fashion.
  </p>
  <p>
    The matrix effect is not a nicety — it is chemistry. Iron from meat (haem iron) is
    absorbed several times more readily than plant (non-haem) iron, and plant absorption
    is further boosted by vitamin C at the same meal and inhibited by tea's tannins — so
    the same iron intake can produce different iron status depending on the company it
    keeps. Fat-soluble vitamins ride with dietary fat: a fat-free salad is, chemically, a
    vitamin A and K loss. Dietary assessment therefore works at both levels. Population
    guidance is set in nutrients — the <em>Dietary Reference Intakes</em> (EAR, the
    requirement meeting half of people; RDA, meeting 97–98%; AI, where an RDA cannot be
    set; UL, the tolerable upper limit) — but counselling is delivered in foods and
    patterns, because compliance lives there. The assessment loop — food record or recall,
    biochemical markers, anthropometry (weight, BMI, waist), clinical signs — is the
    dietitian's clinical method. Deficiency and excess look alike in diets and differ only
    in the data; the discipline exists to keep that distinction honest.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why can the body stockpile years of energy as fat but not even a week of protein?</li>
    <li>Why can high doses of vitamin A become dangerous, while the same megadose of vitamin C mostly washes out?</li>
    <li>A crash diet eliminates nearly all fat. Name two distinct problems that follow within weeks.</li>
    <li>A surplus of 200 kcal a day suggests about 9 kg gained in a year. Why is the real number usually different?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Adipose tissue is a dedicated, high-density store
    (9 kcal/g); protein has no storage form — it does its job as structure and enzymes, and
    surplus amino acids are burned or converted, with their nitrogen excreted as urea.
    (2) A is fat-soluble: absorbed with fat and stored in liver and fat tissue, so chronic
    megadoses accumulate toward toxicity. C is water-soluble: little is stored and the
    excess leaves in urine. (3) Fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K stop being absorbed
    (they ride with dietary fat), and the essential fatty acids (linoleic and
    alpha-linolenic) disappear from the diet — the body cannot make either. (4) Because
    the body adapts: metabolic rate shifts partially with intake and spontaneous activity
    changes, so the static arithmetic overshoots long-term change — the equation gives
    direction and rough magnitude, not an exact forecast.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Six nutrient classes: carbohydrate, protein, fat (energy and material); vitamins, minerals (chemistry); water (medium).</li>
    <li>Energy density: 4, 4, 9 kcal/g — the label "calorie" is a kilocalorie.</li>
    <li>"Essential" means the body cannot synthesise it: nine amino acids, two fatty acids, all vitamins, a set of minerals.</li>
    <li>Fat-soluble vitamins are stored (toxicity possible); water-soluble ones are not (needed regularly, destroyed by overcooking).</li>
    <li>Micronutrients run in windows — both ends of the curve are disease.</li>
    <li>Sodium guards the water outside cells, potassium the inside; sweat takes both water and sodium.</li>
    <li>Energy balance is real thermodynamics but adaptive physiology: use it for direction, not prophecy.</li>
    <li>Nutrients work together: absorption depends on the company a nutrient keeps at the meal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    With the nutrient vocabulary in place, the next lessons follow food on its journey:
    digestion and absorption (how the package is unpacked), life-stage nutrition (what the
    windows look like in pregnancy, childhood and age), and the diet-disease link — how
    patterns, not single nutrients, drive the chronic conditions dietitians counsel
    against. This page gave you the parts list; the rest of the subject is how the parts
    are assembled into meals, plans and clinical decisions.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper currents run under the fundamentals. First, <strong>bioavailability and
    regulation</strong>: the body controls many nutrients at the absorption or excretion
    step (iron's absorption block, calcium's vitamin-D gate, sodium's kidney dial), so
    "intake" and "status" are related through a regulator — the reason supplement doses
    and food doses are not interchangeable, and why serum levels, not labels, are the
    clinical endpoint.
  </p>
  <p>
    Second, <strong>the gut microbiome</strong>: fibre's fermentation products
    (short-chain fatty acids) nourish colon cells and modulate immunity and appetite
    signalling, making the microbiota a quasi-organ whose diet <em>is</em> our diet — the
    most active research frontier in nutrition science, and the mechanistic bridge between
    "eat your fibre" and measurable physiology.
  </p>
  <p>
    Third, <strong>guidance in an age of noise</strong>: single-nutrient headlines ("fat is
    bad" → "sugar is bad") keep oversimplifying the actual evidence structure —
    randomised trials on hard endpoints are rare for diet, cohort studies show
    associations confounded by everything else, and effects are small and
    pattern-dependent. The professional stance is unheroic and stable: adequacy
    (DRI-style windows), variety, energy balance, pattern-level evidence — the same six
    nutrient classes this lesson began with, held together by arithmetic rather than
    enthusiasm.
  </p>

